<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>Everyone waits<br />for the previous word.</h2>
  <p><strong>Recurrence</strong> means passing a running state from one position to the next. It gives a network a way to carry context, but creates a queue: a later state needs the earlier one. Even with the whole training sentence available, that chain cannot be computed all at once.</p>
  <p>By 2016–2017, researchers were exploring ways to work on many positions together. Could you keep useful connections between words while removing the queue?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Change the wiring.</h2>
  <p>A <strong>convolution</strong> applies the same small window of computation at each position. Windows in one layer can run together; stacking layers spreads information farther. Gehring and colleagues’ <strong>ConvS2S (2017)</strong> used convolution in both the input-reading encoder and output-writing decoder, with attention letting each decoder layer consult the input.</p>
  <p><strong>ByteNet (2016)</strong> widened its reach using <strong>dilation</strong>: spacing out the positions a window reads. Parikh and colleagues took another route in 2016: <strong>attention</strong>, a weighted read from other positions. Their model aligned and compared two sentences to decide whether one supported, contradicted, or left the other undecided, without a recurrent sentence reader.</p>
  <p>These were different answers to the same bottleneck. Parallel computation means doing independent work together. It does not mean every architecture does the same work, or that a translator can generate all its output words at once.</p>
