<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play">
  <p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>Place<br />your bet.</h2><ChapterBrief slug="drop-recurrence" />
  <p>Four races, from 8 to 64 words. Before each one, bet which wiring gets word 1’s message to the last word first. Fewer steps help, but every step has to fit through the same chip.</p>
  <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to race. In this game, attention wins the 8- and 16-word races in one step; at 32 and 64 words its all-pairs reads grow so large that the widening convolution stack arrives first.</p></noscript>
  <RecurrenceToy client:load />
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>The queue<br />was a choice.</h2>
  <p>These systems showed that useful sentence processing could work without recurrent state chains. The Transformer paper explicitly compared convolution’s paths with attention’s direct connections, then made self-attention—attention within one sequence—its central mixing operation. <a href="/chapters/transformer/">Try that next step ↗</a></p>
  <aside class="chapter-companions"><h3>Also in this chapter</h3><p>Parikh’s decomposable attention and ByteNet take the two alternative routes above. Earlier, <strong>End-To-End Memory Networks (2015)</strong> made repeated attention reads over stored inputs; <strong>Cheng, Dong and Lapata (2016)</strong> combined attention with a recurrent reader. Attention and recurrence could coexist.</p></aside>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>Windows, alignment and generation</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body">
  <h3>ConvS2S: gates and positions</h3>
  <p>ConvS2S adds learned position vectors to word vectors. A width-k convolution produces two groups of numbers A and B; a gated linear unit computes <code>A ⊙ sigmoid(B)</code>, scaling A by values between zero and one. Residual connections add the layer’s input to its output. Each decoder layer also makes a separate attention read over encoder representations.</p>
  <p>For L undilated layers of width k, the receptive field—the input span that can affect a position—is <code>R = 1 + L(k − 1)</code>. Table 1 reports 40.51 BLEU for a single English–French ConvS2S model. BLEU measures overlap with reference translations, not general understanding.</p>
  <h3>ByteNet: widen the window’s spacing</h3>
  <p>ByteNet’s dilation schedule repeats 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. Each cycle gathers a wider context while retaining a representation at every position. Its decoder sits above the encoder without the usual attention pooling. For a width-three stack with dilation d at each layer, <code>R = 1 + 2 Σ d</code>; doubling spacing expands this span quickly within a cycle. The toy repeats this spacing schedule; it illustrates connectivity, not the complete translation architecture.</p>
  <h3>Parikh: attend, compare, aggregate</h3>
  <p>For word vectors aᵢ and bⱼ from two sentences, a shared feed-forward network F gives scores <code>eᵢⱼ = F(aᵢ)ᵀF(bⱼ)</code>. A softmax turns scores into positive weights summing to one. Weighted reads align the sentences; another network compares each word with its aligned read, then pooled comparisons yield a classification. The base model ignores sentence order. An optional attention step within each sentence adds limited order information. Table 1 reports 86.3% and 86.8% SNLI test accuracy respectively; this is a sentence-pair classification task, not text generation.</p>
  <h3>Parallel training, sequential generation</h3>
  <p>During training, the expected earlier output words are already known. Masked convolutions hide later words, letting positions be trained together without exposing the answer. During generation, the next input includes the word just produced, so prediction still proceeds in sequence. Full attention also scores every pair of positions: for n positions that is n² scores per head. Shorter dependency paths can cost more connections.</p>
  <h3>What the race leaves out</h3>
  <p>A step is one dependent update: the chain passes one state rightward, each convolution layer reads three positions with ByteNet’s widening spacing, and attention reads every pair once. A tick is one round of up to 128 reads, so a step with more reads takes more ticks. The race counts reads and waiting, not embedding sizes, learned weights, gates or translation quality. Real hardware does far more work per tick, which moves the crossover; the trade between short paths and total work stays.</p>
</div></details>
