  <li><a href={sourceLink('kalchbrenner-2016-arxiv-v2-full-text')}>Kalchbrenner et al. · Neural Machine Translation in Linear Time (ByteNet)</a><p>Introduction and Sections 2–3: serial recurrent paths, the stacked encoder and decoder, dilation cycles, context growth, parallel training and autoregressive generation. <a href={sourceLink('kalchbrenner-2016-arxiv-record')}>The arXiv record</a> dates the first submission to 2016.</p></li>
  <li><a href={sourceLink('parikh-2016-acl-full-text')}>Parikh et al. · A Decomposable Attention Model for Natural Language Inference · 2016</a><p>Introduction and Sections 3–4: sentence-pair inference, attend–compare–aggregate, the alignment equation, independent position computation, order-free base model and optional intra-sentence attention. Table 1: both SNLI test accuracies. Bibliographic heading: publication year.</p></li>
  <li><a href={sourceLink('sukhbaatar-2015-neurips-full-text')}>Sukhbaatar et al. · End-To-End Memory Networks · 2015</a><p>Abstract and Section 2: differentiable attention reads over stored inputs, repeated memory hops and a recurrent query update.</p></li>
  <li><a href={sourceLink('cheng-2016-acl-full-text')}>Cheng, Dong &amp; Lapata · Long Short-Term Memory-Networks for Machine Reading · 2016</a><p>Abstract and Section 3: a left-to-right recurrent reader using attention over earlier memory states. The heading gives the publication year.</p></li>
  <li><a href={sourceLink('vaswani-2017-neurips-full-text')}>Vaswani et al. · Attention Is All You Need · 2017</a><p>Sections 1–2: sequential constraints and the explicit comparison with ByteNet, ConvS2S and earlier attention models. Sections 3–4 and Table 1: self-attention, short paths, quadratic pair scoring and parallel position computation. Decoder masking and the output probability factorization explain why ordinary generation remains sequential.</p></li>
